  <h2>What a pyramid is</h2>
  <p>In an ascending pyramid, each set is heavier and has fewer reps than the one before: for example 12, 10 and 8 reps at increasing weights. A straight set (also called constant load) repeats the same weight and rep target for every set. Pyramids are popular because they let you warm up into your working weight, and because they feel productive.</p>

  <h2>What two trials found</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Resistance-trained older women (Ribeiro and colleagues, 2018).</strong> Thirty-three women with an average age of about 70 were randomly assigned to constant load (3 sets of 8 to 12 reps at the same weight) or an ascending pyramid (3 sets of 12, 10 and 8 reps with rising loads). They trained three days a week for 8 weeks after 24 weeks of conditioning. Chest press strength and muscle mass improved in both groups, with no difference between them. The authors concluded both systems work and the pyramid was not superior.</li>
    <li><strong>Well-trained men (Angleri and colleagues, 2022).</strong> In a within-subject design, 15 well-trained men were compared with themselves on a crescent pyramid and on traditional training. Muscle cross-sectional area rose about the same with both (7.5 percent and 7.8 percent), and so did leg press strength (27.3 percent and 26.3 percent). Looking at individuals, 10 of the 15 gained about the same leg press strength either way, four gained more with the pyramid and one gained more with straight sets.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Neither trial tested the barbell bench press, and both are small, so this is not the last word. The honest summary is that the set pattern is a minor variable.</p>

  <h2>What matters more</h2>
  <p>In his coaching interview, Jordan said strength work is task driven: a certain weight for a certain number of reps. The more you go to failure in strength training, the more it works against you and the more you plateau, so you want to complete the programmed reps. For muscle growth, you work close to failure to get mechanical tension. His rep ranges are 3 to 5 for strength and typically 6 to 12 for hypertrophy. Whether you pyramid or not, those decisions do the work.</p>

  <h2>Where a pyramid is useful</h2>
  <p>These uses are general coaching practice, not findings from the trials above.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Warming up into a top set.</strong> Ramping up in weight prepares you for the heavy work and lets you check how the bar feels.</li>
    <li><strong>Finding the day's weight.</strong> If you train by RPE, a short climb shows you what weight gives the effort you want. See <a href="/rpe-bench-press-program/">the RPE bench press program</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Variety.</strong> Some lifters stay more engaged with a pattern that changes across sets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to run one on the bench</h2>
  <p>An example for a lifter with a 275 lb max, aimed at muscle size: 12 reps at 165 lb, 10 reps at 185 lb, 8 reps at 205 lb, with the last set close to the edge of what you can do. For strength, keep the reps low and stop shy of failure: 5 reps at 205 lb, 3 reps at 235 lb, 3 reps at 245 lb. These numbers are only an illustration, so adjust them to your own max and recovery.</p>
